00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.800
And then there is Friday
as an office hour.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.070
I haven't specified
the time for this yet.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.110
I sent you a Doodle poll.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.510
And we'll find the time
which works for most of you

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:05.740
on a Friday afternoon
or Friday morning.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.890
This class doesn't have
a final, so it basically

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.720
ends with the last
day of class, which

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:20.750
is oral exams in the
15th week of this class.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.570
Looking a little bit
into the content,

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.090
the first week is
really an introduction

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.500
and some historic remarks.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:32.690
We'll talk about
relativistic kinematics.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.510
And then we'll go
along the outline as

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.820
given in one of our textbooks.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.250
Again, textbooks are
being discussed yet

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:43.760
in a different
short presentation.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.580
But I really follow
along this textbook,

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:52.020
but not stick too much to only
the content of that textbook.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.170
And I will supplement
the information

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:56.330
using other textbooks,
as well, specifically

00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:00.560
when it comes to some of the
problems and discussions which

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.820
come along.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.880
You can see that we'll
start with particle physics,

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.530
talk about quarks,
leptons, and interactions,

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.195
talk about how this fits
in a theoretical way

00:04:11.195 --> 00:04:14.030
with invariance principle
and conservation laws.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.690
And we'll look at
scattering, and then

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:19.399
QCD before we go into
weak interaction.

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:21.620
And from there on, we
build the standard model

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.510
with the electroweak
interaction, the Higgs

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.313
mechanisms.

00:04:25.313 --> 00:04:27.230
For week eight, then,
or the end of week eight

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:30.080
will give us a break,
where we then start

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.540
talking about nuclear physics.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.390
This order of talking about
particle physics and then

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:39.110
nuclear physics makes
a lot of sense to me.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:42.230
It doesn't make sense
in historic context,

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:44.390
because nuclear
physics have developed

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:47.160
before the standard model,
for example, was developed.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.240
But in order to fully
appreciate nuclear physics,

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.330
it's useful to have
introduced basic interactions.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:57.600
And therefore, this is
the reason for this order.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.380
And then we'll try to bring
in experimental methods

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:04.670
with accelerators, colliding
beams, and experimental methods

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:08.510
on how particles interact
with matter and detectors.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:14.640
Again, in this schedule, the
Thanksgiving week is off.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.640
And we'll use the
last remaining week

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.890
to discuss physics
beyond the standard model

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.620
and the connection to cosmology.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.860
I said here the title the
schedule will be tuned.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:31.130
I very much expect that we have
a little bit slippage in this,

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.690
but there is some
room at the end

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:34.670
to make sure that
we don't overrun,

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:37.100
and that I don't give you
too much to learn, to read,

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:43.160
to watch movies, or do
PSets as we go week by week.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:47.525
This is it for this
first recording.

00:05:47.525 --> 00:05:50.840
As always, you can reach out
and ask any sort of question,

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:55.570
either during the recitation,
office hours, or separate.

